{
  "dom_id": "step-q110056",
  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "The volumes of some chocolate muffins before cooking are normally distributed with a mean of $57\\,\\text{ml}$ and a standard deviation of $2\\,\\text{ml}.$ The volumes of some pumpkin muffins before cooking are normally distributed with a mean of $42\\,\\text{ml}$ and a standard deviation of $1\\,\\text{ml}.$  During cooking, it is known that chocolate muffins double in volume, while pumpkin muffins triple in volume.\n\nIf a chocolate muffin and a  pumpkin muffin are randomly selected after cooking, what is the probability that the sum of their volumes is more than $240\\,\\text{ml}$ but less than $250\\,\\text{ml}?$ You may assume that the volumes of the muffins are independent. \n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9772\\,$ | $\\,0.9778\\,$ | $\\,0.9783\\,$ | $\\,0.9788\\,$ | $\\,0.9793\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9821\\,$ | $\\,0.9826\\,$ | $\\,0.9830\\,$ | $\\,0.9834\\,$ | $\\,0.9838\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.9861\\,$ | $\\,0.9864\\,$ | $\\,0.9868\\,$ | $\\,0.9871\\,$ | $\\,0.9875\\,$ |",
